    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d97">

                <pb facs="0293" n="271" xml:id="pg271"/>

                <head>97. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in the Soviet Union<anchor n="0"
                        xml:id="d97-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1963-04-23T15:24:00-05:00">April 23, 1963,
                            3:24 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>2273. Eyes only Ambassador and Harriman.
                    Following is text of message for Harriman
                    to deliver to Khrushchev:</p>
                <p>“Dear Mr. Chairman:</p>
                <p>I have become most concerned over recent developments in Laos. As you know, I
                    have always regarded our mutual commitment in Vienna to bring peace to that
                    unhappy country as an important milestone in Soviet/American relations. If we
                    could work successfully together to make Laos neutral, we could, I have thought,
                    make progress in resolving other matters which are at issue between us.</p>
                <p>It is because of this concern that I have asked Governor Harriman to make a special trip to confer with
                    you as my representative on this particular aspect of our relations.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d97-f2"/> You and your colleagues have known him well and I
                    put great hope in the results of his discussions with you. I will be awaiting
                    Governor Harriman’s report with keen
                    interest.</p>
                <p>Sincerely”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Rusk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d97-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d97-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Presidential Correspondence: Lot 66 D 204. Secret;
                    Operational Immediate. Drafted and approved by Thompson and cleared in draft by Rusk and at the White House. Repeated to London. Another copy of
                    this message is in the Kennedy Library, National Security Files, Countries
                    Series, USSR, Khrushchev Correspondence.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d97-f2" n="1" xml:id="d97-f2-content">
                <p>For a memorandum of Harriman’s
                    conversation with Khrushchev, see <ref target="frus1961-63v24#pg_1000">vol.
